          "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"CORE_ONLY\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The entry’s distinguishing core—good eye as generosity and evil eye as withholding—is better supported than the broader counter-reading for the cited good/evil-eye texts. Proverbs 22:9 explicitly defines the bountiful or good eye by giving bread to the poor, while Proverbs 23:6 supplies the opposed evil-eye idiom. These passages give Matthew 6:22-23 a concrete Hebrew-scriptural background, reinforced by its treasure-and-mammon context. The broader spiritual-perception reading remains valid at the level of the lamp, light, and darkness imagery, but it does not explain the good/evil-eye wording as specifically as the generosity/stinginess interpretation does. However, the entry’s additional identification of God’s fiery eyes with righteous watchers who shine like stars is not adequately established: Revelation 5:6 explicitly identifies the seven eyes with the seven Spirits of God, Zechariah calls them the eyes of the LORD, and Revelation 1:14 describes Christ’s own fiery eyes. Identifying these with righteous human or created watchers requires chapter-level symbolic transfers not stated by those texts.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"In the good-eye/evil-eye saying, the eye is the body’s lamp and discloses inward moral light, with a good eye concretely expressed as generosity and an evil eye as withholding or stinginess.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The eye is the lamp of the body and reveals the light within.\",\n    \"God’s fiery eyes are His righteous watchers.\",\n    \"Those eyes or watchers range throughout the earth.\",\n    \"Those eyes or watchers shine like the stars in heaven.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"God’s fiery eyes are His righteous watchers; the cited texts identify them more directly as Christ’s eyes, the eyes of the LORD, or the seven Spirits of God.\",\n    \"The fiery eyes or righteous watchers shine like stars in heaven; this identification is not stated in the entry’s cited passages and depends on an extended chapter-level chain of symbolism.\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter’s identification of stars with the righteous does not by itself establish that Christ’s fiery eyes are those righteous persons.\",\n    \"The sun-Father, moon-Son, moon-bride, and relational relay scheme depends on several typological transitions that are not explicit in the cited texts.\",\n    \"The tribe-to-zodiac correspondences and the interpretations of particular planets rely heavily on visual resemblance and extra-biblical astronomical associations.\",\n    \"Arguments based on the late Hebrew Revelation tradition require independent textual justification before they can control New Testament interpretation.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The healthy or single eye in Matthew 6 signifies undivided spiritual perception and allegiance, while the evil eye signifies a morally dark or misdirected inner person; references to God’s eyes separately signify divine omniscience, judgment, or the activity of the Spirit rather than righteous watchers.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The human eye primarily represents spiritual perception and undivided moral orientation, of which generosity may be one contextual manifestation rather than the eye’s defining meaning.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter does not materially exclude the book’s generosity identification; it subsumes generosity within a broader account of moral orientation. Its real denial concerns the degree of semantic specificity, not whether the good eye can be manifested by giving.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Matthew 6:22-23 itself speaks directly of light, darkness, and the condition of the whole person, naturally supporting spiritual perception or moral orientation.\",\n    \"The traditional sense of 'single' can denote sincerity or undivided allegiance rather than generosity alone.\",\n    \"The wider biblical eye symbolism includes enlightened understanding, blindness, desire, and divine watchfulness, demonstrating that 'eye' is polyvalent.\",\n    \"Zechariah 4:10 and Revelation 5:6 identify the roaming eyes with the LORD and the seven Spirits, while Revelation 1:14 presents the fiery eyes as Christ’s own.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Proverbs 22:9 directly explains the good or bountiful eye through giving bread to the poor.\",\n    \"Proverbs 23:6 supplies the matching evil-eye idiom and associates it with a person whose apparent provision should not be trusted.\",\n    \"The generosity/stinginess contrast fits Matthew 6:19-24’s surrounding concern with treasure and mammon more precisely than a generic perception reading.\",\n    \"The book preserves the lamp imagery by treating generosity or withholding as disclosure of the inward light or darkness.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It accounts naturally for Matthew’s explicit vocabulary of light, darkness, and the whole body.\",\n    \"It accommodates the many biblical passages where eyes symbolize understanding, receptivity, blindness, or desire.\",\n    \"It reads the divine-eye passages according to their explicit identifications as God’s sight, Christ’s eyes, or the seven Spirits without introducing righteous watchers.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Proverbs 22:9\",\n    \"Proverbs 23:6\",\n    \"Matthew 6:22-24\",\n    \"Zechariah 4:10\",\n    \"Revelation 1:14\",\n    \"Revelation 5:6\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"Matthew’s word 'single' can plausibly emphasize sincerity or undivided allegiance, so generosity is best treated as the saying’s concrete contextual force rather than an exhaustive meaning of eye.\",\n    \"Proverbs 23:6 establishes an evil-eye host but does not state withholding as explicitly as Proverbs 22:9 states giving.\",\n    \"The asserted identity between God’s fiery eyes and righteous star-like watchers remains unsupported by the printed references.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n}"
